      "marker": "WordPress restorer: local worker recovery; no backdoor input",
      "identification": "Visible WordPress worker snapshot/restoration module, 23 lines of unobfuscated PHP.",
      "execution": [
        "It stores the fixed mu-plugins/cache-worker.php source in shared-memory key 87321 and a gzip/base64 WordPress option. plugins_loaded priority 0 calls restore_worker.",
        "If the worker file is missing, it reads shared memory and writes only a source beginning with <?php, invalidating OPcache. A second missing-file branch reads and decodes the database snapshot. It includes the same fixed path.",
        "The snapshot originates in local file_get_contents of that worker. There are no request parameters, remote destinations, authentication shortcuts, admin-list filters, hidden filenames, permission changes, or alternate injected body in this sample."
      ],
      "verdict_evidence": "The shown behavior is local worker recovery and execution, plausibly a repair helper. Redundant backup and early inclusion warrant the retained suspicious trait, but do not establish a hidden backdoor. The earlier hostile matcher did not even require file recreation or inclusion, much less hiding or attacker-controlled input.",
